   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">-% Status of gender mainstreaming in Economic Sector Development Plans

-	# of UN supported micro, small and medium enterprises recording increase in volumes and incomes from their products, improved market access (local, regional and/or international) and lasting business linkages

-	# of women and youth who report an increase in their` income levels as a result of UN supported initiatives

-	# of private sector institutions financing gender sensitive LED projects</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2017">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strategies to address local bottlenecks and barriers to women&#x2019;s economic empowerment developed
Baseline: 0

Targeted enterprises (individual, cooperatives) both in host community and in refugee population enabled to expand, add value to their products and move up the value chain across the country with a focus on women.
Baseline: 0

Local capacity established to increase the ability of the private sector to finance gender sensitive LED projects
Baseline: 0</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Strategies to address local bottlenecks and barriers to women&#x2019;s economic empowerment developed
2. Targeted YWEE groups in host communities enabled to expand in add value to their products and move up the value chain across the country with a focus
3. Local capacity established to increase the ability of the private sector to finance gender sensitive LED projects</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Capacity building was done to 24 YWEE groups using ILO SYIB training and SIDO entrepreneurship training and food products value addition in accordance to each of the 24 groups choice of economic activities 
2. Completion of Installation of food processing machines to 14 YWEE groups including completion of wiring to the respective business premises
3. A total of 534 men and women benefited from business start-up capital and capacity building in furthering their economic activities; of which 407 females and 127 men from the 4 LGAs. Whereby out of this number a total of 147 were youths out of which 56 belong to groups comprising of both youth and women.  
4. Completion and launching Kigoma Investment guide</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
